# The list surface: indexing, slicing, `append`, sorting where it is, and
# `reverse`.
#
# DIVERGENCE IN SPELLING, NOT IN BEHAVIOUR. This was core/08_lists until the
# in-place `list.sort(key=, reverse=)` was cut. Sorting a list where it is, is
# `xs.sort_in_place(f)` now: the key function is required and positional, as it
# is for `sort_by`, so there is one spelling of a keyed sort per direction and
# no argument that means "the elements themselves" by being absent. CPython has
# no `sort_in_place`, so the file can no longer be oracled, and it carries a
# twin (73_lists.twin.py) instead — this program with `xs.sort()` written back
# in. The .expected below is that twin's output:
#
#   python3 corpus/divergence/73_lists.twin.py \
#     | sed -E 's/\bTrue\b/true/g; s/\bFalse\b/false/g; s/\bNone\b/null/g' \
#     | diff - corpus/divergence/73_lists.expected
#
# An empty diff is the review, so every answer is still CPython's.
#
#   xs.sort()  ->  xs.sort_in_place(x => x)
xs = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6]
print(len(xs))
print(xs[0], xs[-1])
print(xs[2:5])
xs.append(99)
print(xs)
xs.sort_in_place(x => x)
print(xs)
xs.reverse()
print(xs)
# `sum(xs)` and `min`/`max` over a sequence moved to
# corpus/divergence/71_collection_methods.oro when the builtins were cut.
nested = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
print(nested[1][0])
